{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-214121",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that a linear congruence $ax≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$ has a unique solution modulo $n$ if and only if $\\text{gcd}(a,n)=1.$\n\nIn the given congruence, we have $a=40$ and $n=333$, where\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(a,n)=\\text{gcd}(40,333)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, our congruence does indeed have a unique solution.\n\nTo find the solution, we need to multiply both sides of the equation by the multiplicative inverse of $40\\,(\\text{mod}\\,333).$ To find the inverse of $40\\,(\\text{mod}\\,333)$, use the extended Euclidean algorithm.\n\nFirst, we apply the forward reduction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}333 & = & 40\\cdot 8 & + & 13 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 40 & = & 13\\cdot 3 & + & 1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for the rightmost terms in the equations above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}13 & =333-40\\cdot 8 \\\\ 1 & =40-13\\cdot 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we back-substitute:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =40-13\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =40-(333-40\\cdot 8)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =40\\cdot 25+333\\cdot (-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can write this result in modulo $333,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}40\\cdot 25+333\\cdot (-3) & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,333) \\\\ 40\\cdot 25 & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,333)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $40\\cdot 25≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,333),$ which means that $25$ is the multiplicative inverse of $40$ (modulo $333$).\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of our original congruence by $25,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}40x & ≡7 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,333) \\\\ 25\\cdot 40x & ≡25\\cdot 7 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,333) \\\\ (25\\cdot 40)x & ≡175 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,333) \\\\ 1\\cdot x & ≡175 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,333) \\\\ x & ≡175 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,333)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x≡175\\,(\\text{mod}\\,333).$",
